<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Hemingway asked for a same-day visit because the back of his right hand has become red, warm and sore. Two days ago, while he was taking a fish off the line on his boat, a large hook went into the back of his hand near the knuckle of the index finger. He pushed the hook through, cut the barb and pulled it out, and rinsed the hand in seawater and then fresh water. Yesterday the skin around it became pink and today the redness is spreading and throbs. He felt warm last night. He has no blisters, no dark or purple skin, no numbness, and he can move all his fingers. He does not know when he last had a tetanus shot. He is on weekly phlebotomy, naltrexone, sertraline 100 mg, amlodipine 5 mg and thiamine, and he has cut down to about two drinks a day. He asked whether he could take something stronger than acetaminophen for the pain. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Fishhook puncture two days ago; redness since yesterday</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Back of the right hand over the index knuckle</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>Two days</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Red, warm, throbbing</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Moderate; pain 5 of 10</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Constant, worse at night</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Wound from a hook in seawater on his boat; iron overload on weekly phlebotomy</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Better with the hand raised</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>Felt warm last night; no blisters, skin darkening, numbness or loss of finger movement</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

