  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Puncture wound of the right hand with early cellulitis after saltwater exposure (L03.113)</content>. Mild cellulitis without abscess, joint or tendon sheath infection, or signs of a deeper infection. Saltwater wounds can carry Vibrio vulnificus, and people with iron overload or liver disease are at much higher risk of severe infection, so treatment covers Vibrio as well as the usual skin bacteria. No retained hook; X-ray not needed. Opioids were not given: naltrexone blocks them, and acetaminophen with elevation should be enough. NSAIDs were avoided because of his drinking and blood pressure. [S]<list><item>Cephalexin 500 mg four times daily and doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for seven days.</item><item>Tdap today.</item><item>Keep the hand raised; clean the wound daily; no fishing or seawater until it heals.</item><item>Acetaminophen up to 2,000 mg a day.</item><item>Phone check with a photograph tomorrow; go to the emergency department at once for blisters, dark or purple skin, rapidly spreading redness, severe pain, fever or feeling faint.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Hereditary hemochromatosis on weekly phlebotomy (E83.110)</content>. Iron overload raises the risk of severe Vibrio and other infections. This week&apos;s phlebotomy is held while the infection is active. [S]<list><item>Skip this week&apos;s phlebotomy; resume next week if the hand is improving.</item><item>Avoid raw oysters and other raw shellfish, and keep wounds out of seawater.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Alcohol use disorder, severe, on naltrexone (F10.20)</content>. Down to about two drinks a day. Doxycycline and cephalexin can be taken with his current drinking, but less alcohol will help healing. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide or self-harm; his guns remain at the club; he has the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline in his phone. [S]<list><item>Continue naltrexone 50 mg daily.</item><item>Drinking goal and liver results to be reviewed at the fourteen-week visit.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Immunizations given today: Tdap vaccine for a tetanus-prone wound; last tetanus booster more than ten years ago [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Guideline basis: ACG Clinical Guideline: Hereditary Hemochromatosis (2019); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); Adult Immunization Schedule by Age (2026); Practice Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Skin and Soft Tissue Infections: 2014 Update by the Infectious Diseases Society of America (2014)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Phone check with a photograph tomorrow. Fourteen-week review as planned. Return or go to the emergency department at once for the warning signs listed. Next scheduled visit 2026-06-08: fourteen-week review: phlebotomy, the decision to stop drinking, mood and blood pressure. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
